{
  "version": 1,
  "note": "Level extras A1: the five cases taught at A1 (nominative, genitive, accusative, instrumental, locative). Dative comes with A2 extras, vocative with B1 (spec 08 §4).",
  "cases": [
    {
      "id": "nominative",
      "name": "Mianownik",
      "nameEn": "Nominative",
      "questions": "kto? co?",
      "level": "A1",
      "summary": "The dictionary form: the subject of the sentence, and the noun after to jest / to są.",
      "uses": [
        {
          "label": "Subject",
          "pl": "Anna pracuje w banku.",
          "en": "Anna works at a bank."
        },
        {
          "label": "After to jest / to są",
          "pl": "To jest mój brat.",
          "en": "This is my brother."
        },
        {
          "label": "Adjective after być",
          "pl": "Kawa jest gorąca.",
          "en": "The coffee is hot."
        },
        {
          "label": "Name after nazywać się",
          "pl": "Nazywam się Tomek.",
          "en": "My name is Tomek."
        }
      ],
      "triggers": {
        "prepositions": [],
        "verbs": [
          "być + adjective",
          "nazywać się"
        ],
        "other": [
          "to jest",
          "to są"
        ]
      },
      "endings": {
        "singular": {
          "masculine": "— (consonant); a few in -a: tata, kolega",
          "feminine": "-a (a few: -i pani, consonant noc)",
          "neuter": "-o / -e / -ę / -um"
        },
        "plural": {
          "masculine personal": "-i / -y / -owie",
          "other": "-y / -i / -e / -a"
        },
        "adjective": {
          "singular": {
            "masculine": "-y / -i",
            "feminine": "-a",
            "neuter": "-e"
          },
          "plural": {
            "masculine personal": "-i / -y",
            "other": "-e"
          }
        }
      },
      "traps": [
        "After być a noun goes into the instrumental: Jestem studentem, not Jestem student. With to jest the noun stays in the nominative: To jest student.",
        "Masculine personal plurals change the last consonant: student → studenci, Polak → Polacy. Not studenty, Polaki."
      ],
      "contrasts": [
        {
          "with": "accusative",
          "rule": "The subject is in the nominative. The direct object is in the accusative: feminine nouns change -a to -ę.",
          "examples": [
            "Kawa jest dobra.",
            "Lubię kawę."
          ]
        }
      ],
      "topicIds": [
        "a1-cases-nominative",
        "a2-cases-nominative-plural"
      ],
      "setTags": [
        "nominative"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "genitive",
      "name": "Dopełniacz",
      "nameEn": "Genitive",
      "questions": "kogo? czego?",
      "level": "A1",
      "summary": "Absence, possession, quantity, and after many prepositions.",
      "uses": [
        {
          "label": "After negation",
          "pl": "Nie mam czasu.",
          "en": "I don't have time."
        },
        {
          "label": "Possession (of whom?)",
          "pl": "To jest samochód Marka.",
          "en": "This is Marek's car."
        },
        {
          "label": "Quantity and containers",
          "pl": "Poproszę butelkę wody.",
          "en": "A bottle of water, please."
        },
        {
          "label": "After do, z, bez, dla, u",
          "pl": "Idę do sklepu.",
          "en": "I'm going to the shop."
        }
      ],
      "triggers": {
        "prepositions": [
          "do",
          "z (from)",
          "od",
          "bez",
          "dla",
          "u",
          "obok",
          "koło"
        ],
        "verbs": [
          "szukać",
          "słuchać",
          "używać",
          "bać się",
          "potrzebować"
        ],
        "other": [
          "nie ma",
          "nie mam, nie lubię …",
          "dużo",
          "mało",
          "kilka",
          "pięć i więcej"
        ]
      },
      "endings": {
        "singular": {
          "masculine": "-a / -u",
          "feminine": "-y / -i",
          "neuter": "-a"
        },
        "plural": {
          "masculine personal": "-ów / -i / -y",
          "other": "-ów / — / -i / -y"
        },
        "adjective": {
          "singular": {
            "masculine": "-ego",
            "feminine": "-ej",
            "neuter": "-ego"
          },
          "plural": {
            "masculine personal": "-ych / -ich",
            "other": "-ych / -ich"
          }
        }
      },
      "traps": [
        "English does not change the object after not, so learners keep the accusative: Nie mam czas ✗ → Nie mam czasu ✓.",
        "Masculine people and animals that end in a consonant always take -a (brata, kota). For things it is -a or -u, and you learn them in pairs (dom – domu, chleb – chleba).",
        "After nie only an object that would be in the accusative changes. The subject stays in the nominative (Kawa nie jest droga), and other cases stay too (Nie pomagam mamie)."
      ],
      "contrasts": [
        {
          "with": "accusative",
          "rule": "A positive sentence takes the accusative. The same sentence with nie takes the genitive.",
          "examples": [
            "Mam samochód.",
            "Nie mam samochodu."
          ]
        },
        {
          "with": "locative",
          "rule": "do + genitive = where to (a shop, a city). w + locative = where you are.",
          "examples": [
            "Jadę do Krakowa.",
            "Mieszkam w Krakowie."
          ]
        }
      ],
      "topicIds": [
        "a1-cases-genitive-basics",
        "a2-cases-genitive-plural-quantities"
      ],
      "setTags": [
        "genitive"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "accusative",
      "name": "Biernik",
      "nameEn": "Accusative",
      "questions": "kogo? co?",
      "level": "A1",
      "summary": "The direct object: the person or thing the action goes to.",
      "uses": [
        {
          "label": "Direct object",
          "pl": "Czytam książkę.",
          "en": "I'm reading a book."
        },
        {
          "label": "Ordering",
          "pl": "Poproszę kawę.",
          "en": "A coffee, please."
        },
        {
          "label": "Where to, after na",
          "pl": "Idę na koncert.",
          "en": "I'm going to a concert."
        },
        {
          "label": "Days of the week",
          "pl": "w sobotę",
          "en": "on Saturday"
        }
      ],
      "triggers": {
        "prepositions": [
          "na (where to)",
          "w (days: w sobotę)",
          "przez",
          "za (in … time)",
          "o (prosić o, pytać o)"
        ],
        "verbs": [
          "mieć",
          "lubić",
          "znać",
          "widzieć",
          "czytać",
          "pić",
          "jeść",
          "kupować",
          "kochać",
          "odwiedzać"
        ],
        "other": []
      },
      "endings": {
        "singular": {
          "masculine (people, animals)": "-a (in -a: tata → tatę)",
          "masculine (things)": "= nominative",
          "feminine": "-ę (pani → panią, noc → noc)",
          "neuter": "= nominative"
        },
        "plural": {
          "masculine personal": "= genitive",
          "other": "= nominative"
        },
        "adjective": {
          "singular": {
            "masculine (people, animals)": "-ego",
            "masculine (things)": "-y / -i",
            "feminine": "-ą",
            "neuter": "-e"
          },
          "plural": {
            "masculine personal": "-ych / -ich",
            "other": "-e"
          }
        }
      },
      "traps": [
        "English marks the object only by its place in the sentence. Polish needs the ending: Lubię kawę, not Lubię kawa.",
        "Masculine people and animals take -a, like the genitive: Mam brata, Mam psa. Things do not change: Mam dom.",
        "After nie the object switches to the genitive: Mam czas. → Nie mam czasu.",
        "Going into a building or a town takes do + genitive, not w + accusative: Idę do szkoły, not Idę w szkołę."
      ],
      "contrasts": [
        {
          "with": "genitive",
          "rule": "Positive sentence: accusative. Negated sentence: genitive.",
          "examples": [
            "Mam samochód.",
            "Nie mam samochodu."
          ]
        },
        {
          "with": "nominative",
          "rule": "A feminine noun ends in -a as the subject and in -ę as the object.",
          "examples": [
            "Książka jest ciekawa.",
            "Czytam książkę."
          ]
        },
        {
          "with": "locative",
          "rule": "na + accusative = where to. na + locative = where. With w, where to is usually do + genitive: Idę do szkoły, Jestem w szkole.",
          "examples": [
            "Idę na pocztę.",
            "Jestem na poczcie."
          ]
        }
      ],
      "topicIds": [
        "a1-cases-accusative",
        "a2-cases-accusative-plural"
      ],
      "setTags": [
        "accusative"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "instrumental",
      "name": "Narzędnik",
      "nameEn": "Instrumental",
      "questions": "z kim? z czym?",
      "level": "A1",
      "summary": "Who or what someone is after być, the company you are in after z (with), and how you travel or pay.",
      "uses": [
        {
          "label": "A job or nationality after być",
          "pl": "Jestem studentem.",
          "en": "I'm a student."
        },
        {
          "label": "Together with, after z",
          "pl": "Poproszę kawę z mlekiem.",
          "en": "A coffee with milk, please."
        },
        {
          "label": "Means of transport",
          "pl": "Jadę do pracy autobusem.",
          "en": "I'm going to work by bus."
        },
        {
          "label": "Paying",
          "pl": "Czy mogę zapłacić kartą?",
          "en": "Can I pay by card?"
        }
      ],
      "triggers": {
        "prepositions": [
          "z (with)"
        ],
        "verbs": [
          "być + noun (a job, a nationality, a role)"
        ],
        "other": [
          "transport: autobusem, pociągiem, samochodem",
          "paying: kartą, gotówką"
        ]
      },
      "endings": {
        "singular": {
          "masculine": "-em (after k, g: -iem)",
          "feminine": "-ą",
          "neuter": "-em (after k, g: -iem)"
        },
        "plural": {
          "masculine personal": "-ami",
          "other": "-ami (a few: -mi, dziećmi)"
        },
        "adjective": {
          "singular": {
            "masculine": "-ym / -im",
            "feminine": "-ą",
            "neuter": "-ym / -im"
          },
          "plural": {
            "masculine personal": "-ymi / -imi",
            "other": "-ymi / -imi"
          }
        }
      },
      "traps": [
        "English uses the same form in \"I am a student\" and \"the student is here\". Polish needs the instrumental after być: Jestem student ✗ → Jestem studentem ✓.",
        "An adjective alone after być stays in the nominative: Jestem zmęczony, not Jestem zmęczonym. A first name in an introduction stays in the nominative too: Jestem Tomek.",
        "After to jest the noun stays in the nominative: To jest student. But: On jest studentem.",
        "\"By bus\" has no preposition: Jadę z autobusem ✗ → Jadę autobusem ✓. z autobusem means \"together with a bus\"."
      ],
      "contrasts": [
        {
          "with": "nominative",
          "rule": "to jest + nominative names someone; być + noun says what someone is and takes the instrumental.",
          "examples": [
            "To jest lekarz.",
            "On jest lekarzem."
          ]
        },
        {
          "with": "genitive",
          "rule": "z meaning with takes the instrumental; z meaning from takes the genitive.",
          "examples": [
            "kawa z mlekiem",
            "Jestem z Polski."
          ]
        }
      ],
      "topicIds": [
        "a1-cases-instrumental-identity",
        "a2-cases-instrumental-full"
      ],
      "setTags": [
        "instrumental"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "locative",
      "name": "Miejscownik",
      "nameEn": "Locative",
      "questions": "o kim? o czym?",
      "level": "A1",
      "summary": "Where someone or something is, after w and na. The locative never stands alone: it always follows a preposition.",
      "uses": [
        {
          "label": "Where, after w",
          "pl": "Mieszkam w Krakowie.",
          "en": "I live in Kraków."
        },
        {
          "label": "Where, after na (the na-places)",
          "pl": "Czekam na przystanku.",
          "en": "I'm waiting at the stop."
        },
        {
          "label": "Events, after na",
          "pl": "Jestem na koncercie.",
          "en": "I'm at a concert."
        },
        {
          "label": "Surfaces, after na",
          "pl": "Telefon jest na stole.",
          "en": "The phone is on the table."
        },
        {
          "label": "At home",
          "pl": "Wieczorem jestem w domu.",
          "en": "In the evening I'm at home."
        }
      ],
      "triggers": {
        "prepositions": [
          "w (where)",
          "na (where)",
          "o (about: mówić o pracy, A2)",
          "po, przy (A2)"
        ],
        "verbs": [
          "mieszkać w / na",
          "być w / na",
          "pracować w / na"
        ],
        "other": [
          "Gdzie? (where?)"
        ]
      },
      "endings": {
        "singular": {
          "masculine": "-e with a softened consonant (Kraków → w Krakowie); -u after k, g, ch and soft consonants (w banku, w hotelu), and in w domu",
          "feminine": "-e with a softened consonant (w szkole, w Polsce); -i / -y after soft consonants (w kuchni, w pracy)",
          "neuter": "-e with a softened consonant (w kinie); -u after k, g, ch (na lotnisku, w łóżku) and after soft consonants (w mieszkaniu); -um does not change (w muzeum)"
        },
        "plural": {
          "masculine personal": "-ach",
          "other": "-ach"
        },
        "adjective": {
          "singular": {
            "masculine": "-ym / -im",
            "feminine": "-ej",
            "neuter": "-ym / -im"
          },
          "plural": {
            "masculine personal": "-ych / -ich",
            "other": "-ych / -ich"
          }
        }
      },
      "traps": [
        "In English the noun after in or at never changes. In Polish it does: Mieszkam w Kraków ✗ → Mieszkam w Krakowie ✓.",
        "Before -e the consonant softens: k → c (w Polsce), g → dz (w Pradze), t → ci (na poczcie), d → dzi (w ogrodzie), r → rz (w biurze), ł → l (w szkole).",
        "dom takes -u: w domu, not w domie.",
        "Some places take na, not w: na poczcie, na dworcu, na przystanku, na rynku, na lotnisku, na uniwersytecie.",
        "The locative says where you are, not where you go: Idę w sklepie ✗ → Idę do sklepu ✓."
      ],
      "contrasts": [
        {
          "with": "genitive",
          "rule": "w + locative = where you are; do + genitive = where you go.",
          "examples": [
            "Jestem w sklepie.",
            "Idę do sklepu."
          ]
        },
        {
          "with": "accusative",
          "rule": "na + locative = where you are; na + accusative = where you go.",
          "examples": [
            "Jestem na poczcie.",
            "Idę na pocztę."
          ]
        }
      ],
      "topicIds": [
        "a1-cases-locative-places",
        "a2-cases-locative-full"
      ],
      "setTags": [
        "locative"
      ]
    }
  ]
}
